1. AI SOLUTION
Plant diseases pose a threat to global food security and agricultural productivity.
Diagnosing Plant diseases takes a lot of time and needs many resources and expertise. 
Farmers and other agricultural practitioners usually check leaves by hand. 
This manual inspection of symptoms is easy to make mistakes,
especially in places that do not have many resources. Smallholder farmers and
agricultural extension officers find it hard to identify Plant diseases quickly because they do not have access to plant pathologists or diagnostic services.
We plan to use the PlantVillage dataset. This dataset contains thousands of plant leaf images that have already been checked by experts.
The dataset is quality and reliable.
We will build a tool that helps practitioners and farmers detect and classify foliar diseases. The tool uses learning for image classification. 
It can recognise 38 Plant diseases across 14 key crop species: Apple, Blueberry, Cherry, Corn,
Grape, Orange, Peach, Bell Pepper, Potato, Raspberry, Soybean, Squash, Strawberry and Tomato.
This AI solution matches South African goals of using AI technologies to build new tools for agriculture.
The solution will lower the workload. 
It will give a path from detection to corrective actions, for Plant diseases that threaten crops.
It will also bring expertise to smallholder farmers who cannot afford or reach expert plant pathologists.

Business Objectives:
 Increase the accuracy of plant disease diagnosis to than 75% using the PlantVillage dataset
 Cut down the time needed for disease diagnosis from days to just seconds
 Boost crop prognosis by detecting diseases early and helping farmers make smart agricultural decisions
 Lower agricultural losses and reduce input costs for farmers while still maintaining high crop quality
 Expand access to plant disease diagnosis in low-resource and rural areas
 Show how AgriScan AI can automate the process of diagnosing plant diseases in agriculture.

Scalability & Growth:
 Build methods that can be applied to a wider range of agricultural imaging tasks such as stem, root and fruit diseases
 Design a system that allows for continuous updates and expansion of the dataset, with regional disease data
 Create a full ecosystem of tools and services that grow from the foundation of the PlantVillage dataset
 Allow the system to connect with IoT sensors for complete plant health monitoring
 Support the detection of multiple diseases at once especially in cases of complex plant infections.

3.BUSINESS SUCCESS CRITERIA
A. Accuracy
The goal is to have least 75%+ accuracy when classifying plant foliar images into the 38 disease categories across 14 crop species. 
The system needs to have precision and high recall for each disease class to make sure the identification is reliable.

B. Time Efficiency
The system needs to process each leaf image and create an analysis report, including detection, classification,
confidence scores and management recommendations in less than 1 second per image (CPU inference).

C. Confidence Score
The system must give a confidence score (%) for each prediction along with a reliability assessment so users understand how certain the diagnosis is.

D. Actionable Insights
The system must provide disease- information including symptoms, prevention strategies and treatment recommendations so farmers can take immediate action.

E. Accessibility
The system must be easy to use for -technical people through simple interfaces (command-line web application) 
and work without internet after the model is trained to help remote areas that have no connectivity.

 4. BUSINESS BACKGROUND
South Africa and the rest of the continent have big problems in agriculture one of the most serious being the very low number of agricultural extension officers and plant pathologists.
The people who are working in these roles are often very busy covering areas with not enough resources. 
This leads to disease diagnosis, wrong identification and missed chances to treat which causes big crop losses and money problems for smallholder farmers.
Smallholder farmers, who are the farmers in Africa can't get or afford expert plant pathologists for disease diagnosis. 
They depend on ways of knowing or wait for help from extension services, which often comes too late. 
Diseases such as Late Blight in potatoes Blight in tomatoes and Cedar Apple Rust in apples can destroy entire harvests if not found early.
AgriAI-Solutions a company that works with AI saw these problems.Created AgriScan AI—an AI platform made to help reduce the workload and mistakes of people working in agriculture by automatically checking leaf images for early signs and classifying plant diseases.
It uses Deep Learning (Convolutional Neural Networks) to figure out the type of disease, across different crops.
This automatic process helps with classification and finding diseases early. It is a tool that gives farmers, agricultural extension officers and researchers confidence when looking at plant health allowing them to act quickly and cut down on crop losses.

5. REQUIREMENTS
 A. Functional Requirements:
 Input plant leaf images in format such as file paths, file objects, PIL images or NumPy arrays
 The system must Preprocess images, which includes resizing each image to 128×128 pixels adjusting contrast and normalizing pixel values
 The system must predict diseases by assigning each image to one of 38 categories across 14 crop species. These categories also contain healthy classes
 The system must generate a report that summarizes the plant species, disease name, confidence score the top three predictions, symptoms, prevention strategies and recommended treatments
 The system must provide management advice for every disease that is detected
 The system must support batch processing allowing multiple images to be processed at once
 The system must distinguish between healthy and diseased foliage and display clear status indicators for each

B. Non-Functional Requirements:
The system must be able to handle least sixty images per minute at the same time demonstrating effective batch processing capability
The system must maintain performance even when image quality varies or lighting conditions change
The system must be ready for expansion to additional hospitals and agricultural institutions
The system must operate offline after the model has been trained with no need for an internet connection
The system must provide user- interfaces for non-technical staff including both command-line and web application options
The system must generate summary reports and visualizations such as confusion matrices and training curves for researchers
The system must be portable and deployable on consumer hardware without needing a specialized GPU for inference
The system must provide reliability assessments that are based on confidence scores






